---
import BaseV2 from '../../layouts/BaseV2.astro';

const title = 'About HumanAverage';
const description =
  'An independent project that compares European countries using figures their own ' +
  'statistical agencies publish, shows its working, and says plainly what it cannot know.';
---

<BaseV2 title={title} description={description} canonical="/about/">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow mono">About</p>
      <h1>A small project with one rule</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Hi, I’m Simeon. I built HumanAverage on my own, around one rule: no number appears on
        this site unless it can be traced back to data somebody else published.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Why it exists</h2>
      <p>
        Search for a European average and you get a number. It is usually correct, and it is
        almost useless on its own — because one figure for a continent hides the thing you
        actually wanted to know: how far apart the countries are, and where you sit between them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Answering that takes the figure for every country rather than the one for the whole, the
        honesty to say which side of it you fall on rather than inventing a precision the source
        does not support, and a check that the numbers survived the trip from the agency to the
        page. That is what this site does, and the <a href="/method/">method page</a> shows
        exactly how.
      </p>

      <h2>Who makes it, and who does not</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>This site is independent.</strong> I am not part of any government, statistical
        agency, university or institution, nobody commissions or reviews what goes on it, and I
        have no relationship with the agencies whose figures I use — Eurostat, the OECD and the
        European Central Bank — beyond downloading what they publish for anyone.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no anonymous “research team” either, and no medical team, research institute or
        editorial department. It is one person. Where software or AI helps prepare the work, I
        remain responsible for the final check, the choice of sources and the decision to publish.
      </p>

      <h2>Editorial principles</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>I never invent a statistic. Every figure comes from a dataset I can point to.</li>
        <li>I say which kind of figure a page shows, and never call something a percentile
        when the source published a single number instead of a distribution.</li>
        <li>I show no percentile where no representative sample exists, however tempting.</li>
        <li>I keep my own calculation separate from results published by others.</li>
        <li>I describe what a comparison means, and what it does not.</li>
        <li>I correct demonstrable errors and say when the method was last checked.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How it is paid for</h2>
      <p>
        HumanAverage is free to use. Advertising may eventually help cover hosting and further
        development. It currently carries no advertising code, and adverts would never change a
        calculation or a conclusion.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If you can show that something here is wrong, that is genuinely useful and it will be
        fixed. Email <a href="mailto:firenature23@gmail.com">firenature23@gmail.com</a>. For a
        question about a specific result, mentioning which page you were on, the country you
        picked and the figure you entered helps.
      </p>

      <div class="limits">
        <p>
          This site is an informational comparison. It is not medical advice, not a diagnosis,
          and not an assessment of your health.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseV2>
